  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Increased capacity of Member States to develop integrated policies and strategies to create decent job opportunities in the digital economy</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Digitalization and artificial intelligence are reshaping labour markets around the world. While these technologies can drive productivity growth, innovation and the creation of new job opportunities, they are also transforming existing occupations, altering skill requirements and rendering some tasks obsolete. The impacts are uneven across countries, sectors, enterprises and workers, potentially affecting groups differently including women.
In response to this rapidly evolving situation, the strategy is to further develop global evidence on the impact of digitalization and AI on labour markets, based on a coherent framework and ILO guidance. The ILO will systemize the different assessment methodologies, which have already been developed and deployed across the Office in response to specific country and regional demands. This work could be supported by an online platform to help ILO staff and constituents select the most appropriate assessment model for their country, sector or institutional context. Building on the assessment and validation process, the platform could also help users translate evidence into policy responses. In addition, efforts will be made to further analyse global trends in AI policies and labour market issues.</narrative>
  </description>
